CH = dict(num=11, tag="Geography · Ch 6", theme="Manufacturing: importance, classification, major industries and pollution",
 title="Manufacturing Industries",
 intro="Manufacturing turns raw materials into goods of higher value. It creates jobs, earns foreign exchange and removes poverty and regional imbalance. This chapter classifies industries, studies the textile, steel, aluminium, chemical, fertiliser, cement, automobile and IT industries, and the pollution and control measures they involve.",
 big_questions=["Why is manufacturing called the backbone of development?", "How are industries classified?", "Where are the major industries located?", "How do industries pollute and how can this be controlled?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Importance of Manufacturing"),
 ("ul", ["It helps modernise agriculture by providing machines, fertilisers and pesticides, and reduces the heavy dependence of people on agricultural income by providing jobs in secondary and tertiary sectors.", "It reduces unemployment and poverty and brings down regional disparities by establishing industries in tribal and backward areas.", "Export of manufactured goods expands trade and brings in **foreign exchange**.", "Countries that transform raw materials into a wider range of valuable finished goods are prosperous. Agriculture and industry are **not separate**: agro-industries boost agricultural production and competitiveness."]),
 ("p", "The contribution of manufacturing to GDP was almost stagnant at about **17 per cent** for a long time, compared with 25-35 per cent in East Asian economies. The **National Manufacturing Competitiveness Council (NMCC)** was set up to improve the growth of manufacturing; the target is to raise the share to 25 per cent."),
 ("h2", "Classification of Industries"),
 ("table", ["Basis", "Types"], [["Source of raw materials", "Agro-based (cotton, woollen, jute, silk, rubber, sugar, tea, coffee, edible oil); mineral-based (iron and steel, cement, aluminium, machine tools, petrochemicals)"], ["Role", "Basic or key industries (supply to others, e.g. iron and steel, copper smelting); consumer industries (sugar, toothpaste, paper)"], ["Capital investment", "Small scale (investment up to a limit) and large scale"], ["Ownership", "Public sector (BHEL, SAIL), private sector (TISCO, Bajaj Auto), joint sector (Oil India Ltd), cooperative sector (sugar mills in Maharashtra, coir in Kerala)"], ["Bulk and weight of raw material and finished goods", "Heavy industries (iron and steel) and light industries (electrical)"]]),
 ("h2", "Major Industries"),
 ("table", ["Industry", "Key points"], [["Cotton textiles", "Ancient industry in India; handspun and handwoven; first successful mill was set up in **Mumbai (1854)**; spinning is in Maharashtra, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu; early concentration due to local cotton, moist climate, port and market. India's weaving sector, decentralised, is a major employer; India is a leading exporter of cotton yarn. Problems: erratic power supply, outdated machinery, low output of labour and stiff competition from synthetic fibres"], ["Jute textiles", "India is the largest producer of raw jute and jute goods and second to Bangladesh in export; most mills are in the **Hugli basin** (West Bengal); factors: proximity of jute-producing areas, inexpensive water transport, railways, roadways, abundant water, cheap labour. Challenges: stiff competition from synthetic substitutes and from Bangladesh, Brazil, Philippines, Egypt and Thailand; demand for jute bags in packaging has been promoted by the government"], ["Sugar", "Second largest producer in the world after Brazil; mills in Uttar Pradesh, Maharashtra, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat; seasonal, so cooperatives suit; shift to the south and west because of higher sucrose content and longer crushing season"], ["Iron and steel", "Basic industry as it provides raw materials for others; heavy, so located near raw materials; inputs are iron ore, coking coal, limestone, manganese and others in a ratio of about 4 : 2 : 1. **Chotanagpur plateau** has the advantage of cheap iron ore, high-grade raw materials, cheap labour and good market. Plants at Jamshedpur (TISCO, private), Bhilai, Durgapur, Rourkela, Bokaro (SAIL, public), Visakhapatnam, Salem, Bhadravati. Steel is produced in the **mini steel plants** as well. India is among the largest producers of crude steel and the largest producer of sponge iron; per capita consumption is low"], ["Aluminium smelting", "Second most important metallurgical industry; light, resistant to corrosion, good conductor; mixed with other metals; used in aircraft, utensils, wires; bauxite is bulky; needs regular power supply and assured bauxite. Plants in Odisha, West Bengal, Kerala, Uttar Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu"], ["Chemical", "Includes organic (petrochemicals, used in synthetic fibres, plastics, dyes, drugs) and inorganic (sulphuric acid, nitric acid, alkalis, soda ash, caustic soda) chemicals; rapidly growing and diversified; contributes about 3 per cent of GDP"], ["Fertiliser", "Nitrogenous (urea), phosphatic and complex; India is the third largest producer of nitrogenous fertilisers; plants in Gujarat, Uttar Pradesh, Punjab, Tamil Nadu, Kerala and Maharashtra; the Green Revolution raised demand"], ["Cement", "Needs bulky, heavy raw materials: limestone, silica, alumina and gypsum; coal and electric power; the first plant was set up in **Chennai in 1904**; after decontrol in 1989 the industry expanded; plants in Gujarat near ports for exports"], ["Automobile", "Trucks, buses, cars, motorcycles, scooters, three-wheelers; liberalisation and FDI after 1991 increased production; centres around Delhi-Gurugram, Mumbai-Pune, Chennai, Kolkata, Lucknow, Indore, Hyderabad, Jamshedpur and Bengaluru"], ["Information technology and electronics", "Includes transistor sets, TVs, telephones, computers; **Bengaluru** is the electronic capital of India; other centres Mumbai, Delhi, Hyderabad, Pune, Chennai, Kolkata, Lucknow, Coimbatore. The IT industry creates jobs, especially for women, and earns foreign exchange through software and BPO exports"]]),
 ("h2", "Industrial Pollution and Environmental Degradation"),
 ("table", ["Type", "Cause and effect"], [["Air", "Gases such as sulphur dioxide and carbon monoxide, smoke from chemical and paper factories and brick kilns, burning of fossil fuels; harm health"], ["Water", "Dumping of organic and inorganic wastes, effluents, dyes, detergents, pesticides, fertilisers, heat from thermal plants; hot water kills aquatic life"], ["Land", "Dumping of solid waste, fly ash, and slag degrade soil"], ["Noise", "Machinery, generators, drills; hearing loss, increase in blood pressure, irritability"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Thermal pollution** occurs when hot water from factories and power plants is drained into rivers and ponds before cooling. **Taj Mahal** is threatened by the **Mathura refinery** and other industries, as acid rain (sulphur dioxide) damages the marble."),
 ("h3", "Control measures"),
 ("ul", ["Minimise use of water by reusing and recycling in two or more successive stages, harvest rainwater, and treat hot water and effluents before releasing them into rivers.", "Use particulate matter-capturing devices like electrostatic precipitators, scrubbers, fabric filters and inertial separators; install smoke stacks and use oil or gas instead of coal.", "Use generators with silencers, machinery redesign, soundproof equipment and noise absorbing material.", "**NTPC** has adopted environment-friendly measures: afforestation, ash utilisation, reuse of waste and compliance with environmental standards."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Why is manufacturing the backbone of development?", "It creates jobs, earns foreign exchange, supports agriculture and reduces poverty."),
  ("Where was the first modern cotton mill set up?", "Mumbai, 1854."),
  ("Why are jute mills in the Hugli basin?", "Nearby jute, cheap water transport, water, labour and railways."),
  ("Name the inputs of the iron and steel industry.", "Iron ore, coking coal, limestone, manganese."),
  ("Which is the electronic capital of India?", "Bengaluru."),
  ("What is thermal pollution?", "Rise in water temperature due to hot discharges."),
  ("Name two measures to reduce industrial pollution.", "Treat effluents before discharge; use precipitators and recycling of water."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Manufacturing drives **growth, jobs and exports**; the NMCC targets a 25 per cent GDP share.", "Industries are classified by raw material, role, investment and ownership.", "Key industries are **textiles, jute, sugar, steel, aluminium, chemicals, fertiliser, cement, automobile and IT**.", "The industry causes **air, water, land and noise pollution**.", "Recycling, treatment, filters and cleaner fuels can control pollution."])
